{"type":"text","timestamp":1792363757400,"sessionID":"ses_eaecd7658ffez1TS37HVQnELsL","part":{"id":"prt_15134720f001BPhTPPSBQX2JCS","sessionID":"ses_eaecd7658ffez1TS37HVQnELsL","messageID":"msg_151345a71001A9IFrGNYsZk9ra","type":"text","text":"## Semantic verdict: PARTIAL\n\n**Exact review target**\n- DOCX SHA-256: `25f33eb58f9401b33d3ed9f56bb0d99985be564677050848a68937bdca51221c`\n- Extracted text SHA-256: `04152746fda290207d324c6ba54b0c80796c05ea2b9961f43378f5c2a06a695a`\n\nThe candidate is semantically strong and largely matches the unique blueprint: binding, official requirement text, PDF page 5 locator, 17a tier explanation, controlled instrument, complete worked sample dataset, denominator separation, findings, recommendations, neighbor boundaries, and reusable forms are present. Quantitative data recomputes correctly. However, one required per-record traceability linkage set is incomplete.\n\n### Verified (no defect found)\n\n- **Binding/scope:** Document ID `0073`, requirement `I17A-E01`, checkbox serial `080`, indicator 17 / 17a, official source PDF page 5, and source hash are consistent with the blueprint and official evidence form. See cover/first-page block and `Table 2`.\n- **17a tiers:** `Table 3` distinguishes level 0 / 1 / 2 and includes the explicit statement that the candidate does not prove actual tier attainment.\n- **Instrument/method:** `Tables 4–5` contain all 8 items and all 5 anchored labels; sections 3.3–3.7 cover open-ended questions, distribution/return/validity rules, nonresponse/exclusion, anonymity/privacy, feedback-log fields, and coding rules.\n- **Worked sample data:** `Tables 7–12` preserve S01–S15, S16 exclusion, item frequencies, means, positive rates, denominators, and overall totals. Recomputation confirms:\n  - 15 valid learner rows × 8 items = 120 valid item responses\n  - total score = 479\n  - overall mean = 3.9917 → 3.99\n  - positive responses = 90 / 120 = 75.00%\n  - returned = 16 / 16 = 100.00%\n  - valid / distributed = 15 / 16 = 93.75%\n  - valid / returned = 15 / 16 = 93.75%\n  - S16 excluded for missing Q4, Q6, Q7, with no imputation\n- **Stakeholder separation:** `Table 13` keeps learner n=15, instructor n=1, HR/training administration n=1, and department managers n=2 out of the learner denominator.\n- **Findings/analysis:** `Table 16` ranks low areas; sections 6.4–6.5 distinguish closed-question scores from multi-stakeholder comments and identify converging/diverging evidence; section 6.6 states bounded limitations and why the example is not real performance evidence.\n- **Recommendations:** `Tables 17–18` provide finding, evidence, proposed change, role-based owner, trigger, tracking measure, and hold/escalation rule; each is framed as a synthetic proposal, not an association decision. Full course-plan/closure artifacts are kept within 0074 scope.\n- **Neighbor boundaries:** `Table 19` and section 8 correctly distinguish 0072 / I16-E03 / checkbox 078 from 0073 / I17A-E01 / checkbox 080 from 0074 / I17A-E02 / checkbox 081. No invented 0074 content or actual cross-document case link is claimed; the handoff key is marked proposed/unverified.\n- **Reusable forms:** Appendices A–H provide the blank questionnaire, logs, analysis tables, decision/follow-up register, evidence locator, and reviewer checklist.\n- **SAMPLE/REAL boundary:** The document consistently labels synthetic data as `SAMPLE/SYNTHETIC` and real evidence as `REAL=UNKNOWN/HOLD`; no approvals, signatures, credentials, or third-party originals are fabricated.\n\n### Material finding\n\n**F1 — Stakeholder feedback records do not fully implement the required per-record linkage fields.**\n- **Locator:** Section 5.2, `Table 15`, rows `T01`, `H01`, `M01`, `M02`; compare `Table 6` (field definitions) and `Table 18` (recommendation matrix).\n- **Issue:** `Table 15` contains only record ID/group/n, feedback, analysis, proposed response, and status. The blueprint requires each stakeholder record to link feedback, evidence basis, analysis, proposed response, status, **responsibility role, timing, follow-up measure, and limitation**. Limitations are partly embedded in analysis text, and role/timing/follow-up appear only in `Table 18` for R-01–R-05 without explicit record IDs. Therefore the record-level traceability chain is incomplete.\n- **Impact:** The report remains usable, but an evaluator cannot trace every stakeholder record directly to its owner, trigger, follow-up measure, and limitation without cross-referencing the recommendation matrix.\n- **Severity:** Material semantic gap; does not invalidate the quantitative data or overall structure.\n\n### Minor operational note\n\n**F2 — Replacement register is external rather than embedded.**\n- **Locator:** Section 10 and `Table 31`.\n- **Issue:** The candidate documents the 264-row `synthetic_replacement_register_R01.csv` schema and category-level locators, but does not embed row-level IDs in the DOCX. This is acceptable only if the register travels with the record; for standalone use, attach or index the register rows.\n- **Severity:** Minor/operational, not a data defect.\n\n### Boundary with other verdicts\n\n- **Semantic verdict (this review):** PARTIAL, solely because of F1.\n- **Visual verdict:** Not assessed here; the separately recorded visual receipt reports PASS for 19 rendered pages. This semantic review does not claim pixel inspection.\n- **Technical status:** The corrected technical review reports PASS; all supplied counts, distributions, means, and percentages recomputed successfully.\n\n**Bottom line:** The document is a mature, directly usable synthetic demonstration record and passes quantitative, boundary, and safety checks, but it should not be treated as fully satisfying the blueprint until `Table 15` is expanded so each stakeholder record explicitly carries responsibility role, timing, follow-up measure, and limitation.","time":{"start":1792363753999,"end":1792363757400}}}
